## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical OAuth2 debugging skills, understanding of token lifecycle, and real-world troubleshooting abilities that backend engineers face daily.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates identify: 1) Refresh token expired/revoked, 2) Scope mismatch between access and refresh tokens, 3) Client configuration issues. They should mention checking token introspection endpoint, reviewing logs, and testing with Postman/curl.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Debug refresh token failure
async function debugRefreshToken(refreshToken: string) {
  try {
    // 1. Check token introspection
    const introspect = await fetch('/oauth/introspect', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: `token=${refreshToken}`
    });

    // 2. Validate client configuration
    const clientConfig = await getClientConfig();

    // 3. Review token storage
    const storedToken = await getStoredToken(refreshToken);

    return { introspect, clientConfig, storedToken };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Debug failed:', error);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests versioned cache invalidation across tenants, balancing consistency and hot-path performance.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant, per-product cache keys with a shared version
- Invalidation via version bump and pub/sub
- Stampede protection with distributed locking
- TTL-based fallback for eventual consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Python-like pseudocode in JS terms; Redis calls shown for clarity
async function updateProduct(pId, price, stock){
  const ver = await redis.incr(`product:${pId}:version`);
  await redis.publish(`prod_updated:${pId}`, String(ver));
}
async function getProductForTenant(pId, tenantId){
  const ver = await redis.get(`product:${pId}:version`);
  const cacheKey = `cache:prod:${pId}:tenant:${tenantId}:v${ver}`;
  let data = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if(data) return JSON.parse(data);
  const lock = `lock:cache:${pId}:${tenantId}`;
  const ok = await redis.set(lock, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5);
  if(ok){
    data = await loadFromDB(pId); // resp: price, stock, etc.
    await redis.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(data), 'EX', 60);
    await redis.del(lock);
  } else {
    await sleep(50);
    return getProductForTenant(pId, tenantId);
  }
  return data;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this approach across regions with cross-region cache coherence?
- How would you monitor invalidation latency and TTL tuning in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CQRS pattern in real-time collaborative apps - crucial for Miro's architecture where immediate UI feedback and data consistency are both required.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidate will explain: Command side receives shape update request, validates permissions, persists to database, emits ShapeUpdated event. Query side maintains read model for fast UI rendering. Event-driven architecture ensures eventual consistency across all connected clients.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Command
class UpdateShapePositionCommand {
  constructor(public shapeId: string, public x: number, public y: number) {}
}

// Command Handler
class ShapeCommandHandler {
  async handle(command: UpdateShapePositionCommand) {
    const shape = await this.repo.findById(command.shapeId);
    shape.updatePosition(command.x, command.y);
    await this.repo.save(shape);

    // Emit event for query side
    this.eventBus.emit(new ShapeUpdated(shape.id, shape.x, shape.y));
  }
}

// Query side optimized for UI
class ShapeReadModel {
  async getShape(shapeId: string) {
    return this.readDb.shapes.find(shapeId);
  }
}
```

